WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.901
A CNC lathe up close.

2
00:00:03.451 --> 00:00:07.552
The chuck spins a steel shaft, and the tool stands still.

3
00:00:08.102 --> 00:00:12.807
It cuts with a small carbide insert, made of very hard carbide.

4
00:00:13.707 --> 00:00:18.154
The cutting speed is how fast the material slides past the cutting edge,

5
00:00:18.354 --> 00:00:19.782
in metres per minute.

6
00:00:20.332 --> 00:00:25.800
Carbide in steel runs at about 150 to 300 metres per minute.

7
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:30.158
But the spindle turns in revolutions per minute.

8
00:00:30.708 --> 00:00:33.692
That spindle speed depends on the diameter.

9
00:00:34.242 --> 00:00:40.871
A 60 millimetre shaft has a circumference of about 188.5 millimetres.

10
00:00:41.421 --> 00:00:49.690
At 220 metres per minute, the spindle turns at 1,167 revolutions a minute.

11
00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:55.643
The thinner the shaft, the faster the spindle turns for the same cutting speed.

12
00:00:56.193 --> 00:01:01.641
So a lathe speeds up as the tool moves towards the centre, up to a set maximum.

13
00:01:02.841 --> 00:01:06.593
As the shaft turns, the tool slides along it.

14
00:01:07.144 --> 00:01:12.728
The feed is how far it moves on per revolution: here 0.3 millimetres.

15
00:01:13.628 --> 00:01:17.889
The depth of cut is how deep the tool goes in: the step in the shaft.

16
00:01:18.789 --> 00:01:25.929
Feed times spindle speed is the feed rate: 0.3 times 1,167,

17
00:01:26.129 --> 00:01:29.124
so 350 millimetres per minute.

18
00:01:29.675 --> 00:01:33.889
A cut of 200 millimetres then takes 34 seconds.

19
00:01:35.089 --> 00:01:37.005
Why not simply cut faster?

20
00:01:37.555 --> 00:01:40.550
Then the edge runs hotter and wears faster.

21
00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:43.631
How long it lasts is its tool life.

22
00:01:44.181 --> 00:01:48.314
Rule of thumb for carbide: 20 percent faster roughly halves it.

23
00:01:49.214 --> 00:01:53.559
More feed costs less tool life, but makes the surface rougher.

24
00:01:54.109 --> 00:01:57.385
So when roughing, you raise the feed first.

25
00:01:57.935 --> 00:02:05.705
At 0.4 it becomes 467 millimetres per minute, and the cut takes 26 seconds.

26
00:02:06.905 --> 00:02:10.806
The tip of the insert is rounded: that is the nose radius,

27
00:02:11.006 --> 00:02:15.894
usually 0.4, 0.8 or 1.2 millimetres.

28
00:02:16.444 --> 00:02:18.696
It leaves a fine helix behind.

29
00:02:19.596 --> 00:02:22.324
The distance between the grooves is the feed.

30
00:02:22.874 --> 00:02:25.196
A larger radius makes them shallower.

31
00:02:25.746 --> 00:02:30.997
And the feed counts squared: halve it, and the roughness Ra drops to a quarter.

32
00:02:31.897 --> 00:02:39.679
With feed 0.25 and radius 0.8, the theoretical Ra is 2.4 micrometres.

33
00:02:40.228 --> 00:02:45.223
At feed 0.14, Ra drops to 0.77.

34
00:02:45.773 --> 00:02:49.944
In practice you often measure more, up to about twice as much.

35
00:02:51.144 --> 00:02:53.524
The metal that comes off is the chip.

36
00:02:54.074 --> 00:02:57.641
The chip breaker, a small shape on the insert,

37
00:02:57.841 --> 00:03:02.650
curls it until it breaks: short curls, like a 6 or a 9.

38
00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:06.975
With too little feed or depth of cut it does not work.

39
00:03:07.525 --> 00:03:11.498
Then you get long, stringy chips that wrap round the part.

40
00:03:12.698 --> 00:03:15.589
A long, thin shaft bends away under the tool.

41
00:03:16.139 --> 00:03:20.702
What counts is its slenderness: the overhang divided by the diameter.

42
00:03:21.252 --> 00:03:25.942
Here 160 divided by 20: 8 times the diameter.

43
00:03:26.842 --> 00:03:28.932
Such a shaft starts to chatter.

44
00:03:29.482 --> 00:03:35.359
Above about 3 times the diameter, the tailstock supports the free end with a centre.

45
00:03:36.259 --> 00:03:38.604
Above about 6 times the diameter,

46
00:03:38.804 --> 00:03:42.415
a steady rest is added: a support with rollers halfway.

47
00:03:42.965 --> 00:03:44.904
So this shaft needs both.

48
00:03:46.104 --> 00:03:50.493
Finally, the turn-mill centre: a lathe that also mills and drills.

49
00:03:51.043 --> 00:03:56.012
Its turret carries driven tools: cutters and drills with their own small motor.

50
00:03:56.912 --> 00:04:00.314
The C-axis stops the spindle at a controlled angle.

51
00:04:00.864 --> 00:04:04.266
That is how you drill six holes 60 degrees apart.

52
00:04:04.815 --> 00:04:08.788
The Y-axis moves the tool off centre, for a straight flat.

53
00:04:09.688 --> 00:04:14.114
The part drops off finished: complete machining, in one set-up.

54
00:04:14.664 --> 00:04:20.585
A flange that took 4.0 plus 3.0 minutes on two machines is done here in 6.0 minutes.

55
00:04:21.485 --> 00:04:26.257
And a bar feeder keeps pushing a bar of about 3 metres a little further in.

56
00:04:26.807 --> 00:04:29.953
So it runs on unmanned after the last shift.
